    <entry xml:lang="en">
        <title>Why single-binary tools age so well</title>
        <published>2026-03-10T00:00:00+00:00</published>
        <updated>2026-03-10T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
        
        <author>
          <name>Jose Garcia</name>
        </author>
        
        <link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://josegarcia.dev/microblog/single-binary-tools/"/>
        <id>https://josegarcia.dev/microblog/single-binary-tools/</id>
        
        <content type="html" xml:base="https://josegarcia.dev/microblog/single-binary-tools/">&lt;p&gt;Every time I come back to a project after six months, the difference between a single compiled binary (&lt;code&gt;zola build&lt;/code&gt;) and a fragile dependency tree is night and day. Tools that do one job without runtime ceremony let you focus entirely on the work itself.&lt;/p&gt;
</content>
        
